/* study-guide-data.js: every unit's Study Guide tab, drawn by study-guide.js.
   STUDY_GUIDE[n] = [{num, title, body}] in display order. num is the small label
   on the right ("Topic 1.1"), title the heading, body the HTML shown when a
   section is opened (<p>, <ul>, .sg-callout, .sg-kws/.sg-kw key-term badges). */
window.STUDY_GUIDE = {
1: [
  {"num":"Topic 1.1","title":"Song China &amp; East Asia","body":"<p>The <strong>Song Dynasty</strong> ruled through a centralized <strong>bureaucracy</strong> staffed by a <strong>meritocracy</strong>: officials earned their posts by passing the <strong>Civil Service Exam</strong>, which tested mastery of Confucian classics. This produced the <strong>scholar-gentry</strong>, a class that even surpassed the aristocracy in status. Because peasants could in theory study and sit the exam, the system offered rare <strong>social mobility</strong>.</p>\n<p>An <strong>economic revolution</strong> followed: fast-ripening <strong>Champa rice</strong> from Vietnam multiplied the food supply and population; the <strong>Grand Canal</strong> knit internal markets together; and China led the world in <strong>proto-industrialization</strong>, iron, steel, porcelain, silk, and tea. Chinese innovations (gunpowder, the magnetic compass, woodblock printing) spread west along the Silk Roads.</p>\n<p>Society was <strong>patriarchal</strong>, emphasizing <strong>filial piety</strong> and Confucian hierarchy; elite women faced the painful status marker of <strong>foot binding</strong>. Belief blended <strong>Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism</strong>, producing <strong>Neo-Confucianism</strong>. Song China projected power through a <strong>tributary system</strong> over neighbors. <strong>Japan</strong> (Heian court, Tale of Genji, then feudal shogunate), <strong>Korea</strong> (Confucian elite, closed exam), and <strong>Vietnam</strong> (least sinified, greater freedom for women) each adapted Chinese models selectively.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Song China is the go-to example of <strong>continuity &amp; innovation in governance</strong>, the civil-service exam is a top comparison point for how states maintained and legitimized power.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Civil Service Exam</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Scholar-gentry</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Neo-Confucianism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Filial piety</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Foot binding</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Champa rice</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Grand Canal</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Tributary system</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.2","title":"Dar al-Islam","body":"<p><strong>Dar al-Islam</strong> (\"the house of Islam\") was culturally unified even as it fragmented politically. The <strong>Abbasid Caliphate</strong> declined and power passed to new Islamic states led by <strong>Turkic</strong> peoples, the <strong>Seljuk Turks</strong>, the Egyptian <strong>Mamluks</strong>, and the <strong>Delhi Sultanate</strong> in India. These states expanded Islam's reach even as no single ruler controlled it all.</p>\n<p>This was a cultural <strong>Golden Age</strong>. The <strong>House of Wisdom</strong> in Baghdad translated and preserved Greek and Latin works, later fueling Europe's Renaissance: while scholars like <strong>Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</strong> advanced astronomy, mathematics, medicine, and law. Adopted <strong>papermaking</strong> from China spread literacy; <strong>Al-Andalus</strong> (Islamic Spain) and its capital <strong>Córdoba</strong> became centers of learning. Poets such as <strong>'A'ishah al-Ba'uniyyah</strong> flourished.</p>\n<p>Islam spread mainly through <strong>trade, missionaries (Sufis), and conquest</strong>, not forced conversion, <strong>protected peoples</strong> (Jews, Christians, Zoroastrians) paid the <strong>jizya</strong> tax rather than convert. Merchants held high status, and women could inherit, divorce, and testify in court, though they remained limited as wives and concubines.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Dar al-Islam is the prime example of <strong>cultural continuity amid political change</strong> and of how <strong>trade routes carried religion and knowledge</strong> across Afro-Eurasia.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Abbasid Caliphate</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">House of Wisdom</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Seljuk Turks</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mamluks</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Delhi Sultanate</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Sufism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Córdoba</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Jizya</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.3","title":"South &amp; Southeast Asia","body":"<p>In <strong>South Asia</strong>, the more stable south hosted the <strong>Chola Dynasty</strong> and later the powerful Hindu <strong>Vijayanagara Empire</strong>, while the north was fragmented and repeatedly attacked by Islamic forces, the <strong>Rajput</strong> kingdoms and the Muslim <strong>Delhi Sultanate</strong>. Islam's arrival produced a new blended language, <strong>Urdu</strong>, and architecture like the <strong>Qutub Minar</strong>. Many lower-caste Hindus converted to Islam to escape the rigid <strong>caste system</strong>, while the devotional <strong>Bhakti Movement</strong> re-energized Hinduism by stressing a direct, personal love of god open to all.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Southeast Asia</strong>, sea-based kingdoms thrived on Indian Ocean trade: the Hindu, navally powerful <strong>Srivijaya</strong>, then the Buddhist <strong>Majapahit</strong>, which controlled key sea routes. Land-based kingdoms included the <strong>Khmer Empire / Angkor</strong>, whose capital Angkor Thom fused Hindu and Buddhist art and whose complex irrigation supported dense rice agriculture.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Use this region to show <strong>religious syncretism and the appeal of Islam and Bhakti to lower castes</strong>, plus how <strong>control of sea routes built state power</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Vijayanagara</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Delhi Sultanate</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Bhakti Movement</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Rajput</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Srivijaya</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Majapahit</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Khmer / Angkor</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Urdu</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.4","title":"The Americas","body":"<p>The <strong>Maya</strong> were organized as independent <strong>city-states</strong> ruled by god-kings (<strong>theocracy</strong>) with no central government; they developed the concept of <strong>zero</strong>, a glyph writing system, and an accurate calendar, and practiced <strong>human sacrifice</strong>. Warfare between city-states was common.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Aztec (Mexica)</strong> built an empire in central Mexico with a capital at <strong>Tenochtitlan</strong>. A <strong>tribute system</strong> collected crops, goods, and sacrificial victims from conquered provinces; <strong>chinampas</strong> (floating gardens) fed the swampy capital; and they practiced human sacrifice on a large scale, which, along with disease and the Spanish, fed rebellion and decline.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Inca</strong> ruled a vast Andean empire divided into four provinces, linked by the <strong>Carpa Ñan</strong> road network. They demanded labor through the <strong>mit'a system</strong> rather than tribute, recorded data with knotted-string <strong>quipu</strong>, and farmed steep terrain with <strong>terrace farming</strong>. Civil war after the emperor's death, plus disease and Spanish violence, brought collapse.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Compare the three: all were <strong>theocratic</strong> and used <strong>labor/tribute systems</strong> for control, but the Inca used <strong>mit'a labor</strong> where the Aztec used <strong>tribute goods</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Maya</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Aztec / Mexica</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Tenochtitlan</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Chinampas</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Inca</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mit'a system</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Quipu</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Carpa Ñan</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.5","title":"Africa","body":"<p>Many African societies began as <strong>kin-based networks</strong> that grew into larger kingdoms, such as the <strong>Hausa Kingdoms</strong>. In West Africa, <strong>Ghana</strong> and then <strong>Mali</strong> grew rich by taxing the <strong>trans-Saharan trade</strong> in gold, ivory, and salt. Mali's rulers <strong>Sundiata</strong> (founder) and <strong>Mansa Musa</strong> (whose lavish pilgrimage to Mecca broadcast Mali's wealth) made <strong>Timbuktu</strong> a center of trade and Islamic learning.</p>\n<p>In East Africa, the <strong>Swahili city-states</strong>, a blend of <strong>Bantu and Arabic</strong> language and culture, linked the coast to Indian Ocean trade. <strong>Great Zimbabwe</strong> built massive stone walls without mortar, and <strong>Ethiopia</strong> remained a Christian kingdom. History was preserved orally by <strong>griots/griottes</strong>. Islam and Christianity spread but often blended with, rather than replaced, <strong>animist</strong> traditions.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Africa is a strong example of <strong>trade networks driving state formation and cultural exchange</strong>, Mansa Musa and the Swahili coast are frequent test subjects.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Ghana</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mali</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mansa Musa</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Sundiata</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Swahili city-states</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Great Zimbabwe</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Hausa</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Griots</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.6","title":"Medieval Europe","body":"<p>Medieval Europe was politically <strong>decentralized</strong> under <strong>feudalism</strong>, a hierarchy of kings, lords, knights, and serfs bound by land and loyalty, and economically organized by the <strong>manorial system</strong>, in which serfs worked self-sufficient estates. The <strong>three-field system</strong> raised agricultural output and population.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Roman Catholic Church</strong> dominated art, education, and daily life; the <strong>Great Schism</strong> split Roman Catholic (West) from Orthodox (East) Christianity. The <strong>Crusades</strong> pitted Christians against Muslims but reopened European interest in Asian goods, an interest travelers like <strong>Marco Polo</strong> intensified. A new merchant middle class (<strong>bourgeoisie / burghers</strong>) emerged, and monarchs began centralizing power, as in <strong>Norman England</strong> with Parliament and the <strong>Magna Carta</strong>.</p>\n<p>Late in the period, the <strong>Black Death</strong> and the <strong>Little Ice Age</strong> weakened feudalism, while the <strong>Renaissance</strong> revived Greco-Roman arts and learning, ending the \"Dark Ages\" and pointing toward the nation-states to come.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Contrast Europe's <strong>decentralized feudalism</strong> with the <strong>centralized bureaucracy of Song China</strong>, a classic governance comparison.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Feudalism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Manorialism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Three-field system</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Great Schism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Crusades</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Black Death</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Bourgeoisie</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Magna Carta</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 1.7","title":"Comparing States &amp; Societies, 1200–1450","body":"<p>Topic 1.7 asks you to <strong>compare</strong> how states across the world gained and kept power. Some were <strong>centralized</strong>: <strong>Song China</strong> ran a bureaucracy staffed partly by merit through the <strong>civil service exams</strong>, and the <strong>Inca</strong> controlled subjects directly with the <strong>mit’a</strong>, roads, and resettlement. Others were <strong>decentralized</strong>: <strong>feudal Europe</strong> depended on hereditary lords, and the <strong>Aztecs</strong> let conquered city-states keep their rulers as long as they paid <strong>tribute</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rulers everywhere used <strong>religion to legitimize power</strong>: the <strong>Mandate of Heaven</strong> and Confucianism in China, Islam in the <strong>Delhi Sultanate</strong> and <strong>Mali</strong>, Hindu and Buddhist temples such as <strong>Angkor Wat</strong>, Christianity and the Church in Europe, and Aztec state religion. Belief systems also shaped <strong>social hierarchies</strong>, from Neo-Confucian patriarchy to the Hindu <strong>caste system</strong> and European feudal orders.</p>\n<p>Trade connected many of these societies. The <strong>Swahili city-states</strong>, <strong>Song ports</strong>, and <strong>Srivijaya</strong> all grew rich from Indian Ocean commerce, while <strong>Mali</strong> prospered from the trans-Saharan gold trade.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Comparison questions want <strong>both a similarity and a difference</strong> explained with specific examples, for example: both Song China and Mali used religion to support rulers, but Song China relied on a Confucian bureaucracy while Mali relied on control of trade.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Centralized vs. decentralized</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Civil service exams</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Feudalism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Tribute</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mit’a</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mandate of Heaven</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Caste system</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Legitimacy</span></div>"}
],
2: [
  {"num":"Topic 2.1","title":"The Silk Roads","body":"<p>The overland <strong>Silk Roads</strong> across Central Asia revived and expanded in this era. Demand drove growth: the <strong>Crusades</strong> gave Europeans a taste for Eastern silks and spices, while new and reviving empires, the <strong>Abbasids</strong>, <strong>Tang and Song China</strong>, and later the <strong>Mongols</strong>, offered desirable goods and made the routes safer.</p>\n<p>New <strong>technologies</strong> and <strong>commercial innovations</strong> fueled the trade: <strong>caravanserai</strong> (inns spaced roughly 100 miles apart), better camel saddles, the magnetic compass, and the Chinese <strong>junk</strong> ship. Crucially, China developed a <strong>money economy</strong> with a form of paper credit called <strong>flying cash</strong>, which inspired the growth of banking. Surging demand for <strong>silk and porcelain</strong> pushed Chinese <strong>proto-industrialization</strong> in iron and steel.</p>\n<p>The effects rippled outward: oasis cities like <strong>Kashgar</strong> and <strong>Samarkand</strong> flourished as trading and cultural hubs; goods such as silk, porcelain, tea, paper, and gunpowder moved west; and religions: <strong>Buddhism, Christianity, Islam, and Zoroastrianism</strong>, traveled with the merchants.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Use the Silk Roads to show how <strong>commercial innovations (flying cash, caravanserai) and state stability increased the volume of trade</strong>, a classic cause-and-effect chain.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Silk Roads</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Caravanserai</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Flying cash</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Money economy</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Samarkand</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Kashgar</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Junk ship</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Proto-industrialization</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.2","title":"The Mongol Empire","body":"<p>The <strong>Mongols</strong> began as pastoral nomadic clans north of the Gobi Desert, prizing warfare, horsemanship, and relative gender equality. <strong>Temujin</strong> united the clans and was proclaimed <strong>Genghis Khan</strong> (\"universal ruler\"). Mongol armies conquered with siege weapons, speed, and terror, annihilating those who resisted but incorporating those who surrendered.</p>\n<p>Once in power, the Mongols governed with <strong>religious tolerance</strong> and cultural inclusion, creating the <strong>Pax Mongolica</strong>, a \"Mongol Peace\" that secured and revitalized trade across Eurasia. Genghis Khan's heirs divided the empire into khanates: the <strong>Yuan Dynasty</strong> in China (Kublai Khan), the <strong>Il-Khanate</strong> in Persia, and the <strong>Golden Horde</strong> in Russia.</p>\n<p>The impact was immense. The Mongols guarded the Silk Roads and accelerated <strong>cultural and technological exchange</strong>, but also spread the <strong>bubonic plague</strong>. Long term: China's scholar-gentry was temporarily dissolved; <strong>Russia</strong> was isolated from Western Europe and later rushed to catch up; and the Islamic world eventually fractured into the <strong>gunpowder empires</strong> (Ottoman, Safavid, Mughal). Rebellions such as the <strong>White Lotus Society</strong> hastened Mongol decline.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>The Mongols are the ultimate example of how a <strong>strong, tolerant empire facilitated trade and exchange (Pax Mongolica)</strong>: connect them to the revival of the Silk Roads and the spread of the plague.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Genghis Khan</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Pax Mongolica</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Yuan Dynasty</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Il-Khanate</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Golden Horde</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Kublai Khan</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Religious tolerance</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">White Lotus Society</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.3","title":"Indian Ocean Trade","body":"<p>The <strong>Indian Ocean</strong> network stretched from Southeast Asia to East Africa and became the world's richest maritime system. Its growth was driven by the <strong>spread of Islam</strong>, which linked port cities like <strong>Calicut</strong>, and by soaring <strong>demand</strong>, cotton and pepper from India, nutmeg, cloves, and cinnamon from the <strong>Spice Islands</strong>, gold, ivory, and enslaved people from the <strong>Swahili coast</strong>, and silk and porcelain from China.</p>\n<p>Success depended on <strong>environmental knowledge and technology</strong>: sailors used the predictable <strong>monsoon winds</strong>, along with the <strong>lateen sail</strong>, the Chinese <strong>stern rudder</strong>, the <strong>magnetic compass</strong>, and the Muslim-refined <strong>astrolabe</strong>.</p>\n<p>Effects included the rise of <strong>diasporic communities</strong>, merchants who settled in foreign ports (often marrying local women) and blended cultures, and the growth of powerful trading states like <strong>Melaka (Malacca)</strong>, which built a navy and taxed passing ships. The <strong>Swahili city-states</strong> boomed, and China projected power through <strong>Zheng He's</strong> massive treasure-fleet voyages.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Indian Ocean trade is the best example of <strong>how environmental knowledge (monsoons) and maritime technology enabled exchange</strong> and produced <strong>diasporic communities and new states</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Monsoon winds</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Lateen sail</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Astrolabe</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Stern rudder</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Diasporic communities</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Swahili city-states</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Zheng He</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Melaka</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.4","title":"Trans-Saharan Trade","body":"<p>Across the Sahara, the <strong>trans-Saharan trade</strong> connected West Africa to North Africa and the Mediterranean world. It was made possible by <strong>technology for desert travel</strong>: the <strong>camel</strong>, specialized <strong>camel saddles</strong> (like the Somali saddle that could carry heavy loads), and <strong>caravans</strong> protected by armed escorts.</p>\n<p>Rising <strong>demand</strong> for <strong>gold, ivory, and enslaved people</strong> expanded the routes and enriched West African states. The <strong>Mali Empire</strong> grew powerful by taxing this trade; strong rulers like <strong>Sundiata</strong> (its founder) and <strong>Mansa Musa</strong>, whose famous pilgrimage to Mecca showered gold across North Africa, built new trading and diplomatic relationships.</p>\n<p>Effects included the growth of wealthy kingdoms with their own currencies and the rise of <strong>Timbuktu</strong> and <strong>Gao</strong> as major centers of <strong>trade and Islamic learning</strong>. Islam spread along the routes, though it often <strong>blended with</strong> rather than replaced existing traditions.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>Trans-Saharan trade shows how <strong>new transport technology (camel saddles) and demand for gold built states like Mali</strong> and spread Islam and learning (Timbuktu).</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Trans-Saharan trade</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Camel saddle</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mali</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Mansa Musa</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Sundiata</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Timbuktu</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Gold–salt trade</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Gao</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.5","title":"Cultural Consequences of Connectivity","body":"<p>The trade networks carried <strong>ideas and religions</strong> as much as goods. <strong>Buddhism</strong> spread from China (blending into <strong>Neo-Confucianism</strong>) onward to Japan and Korea, and <strong>Buddhism and Hinduism</strong> spread to Southeast Asia, shaping Srivijaya, Majapahit, and the Khmer/Angkor kingdoms. <strong>Islam</strong> spread across Africa, South Asia, and Southeast Asia, appealing to lower-caste Hindus and producing blended architecture, the <strong>Urdu</strong> language, and new artistic forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Technological and intellectual transfers</strong> reshaped societies: Greek texts were translated into Arabic (preserving them), <strong>mathematics</strong> spread from India, and <strong>papermaking and printing</strong> from China raised literacy. <strong>Champa rice</strong>, maritime technologies, and <strong>gunpowder</strong> all diffused along the routes.</p>\n<p>Famous <strong>travelers</strong> documented this connected world: <strong>Marco Polo</strong> sparked European fascination with China's cities; <strong>Ibn Battuta</strong> recorded the Muslim world from a devout POV; and <strong>Margery Kempe</strong> left a rare firsthand account of a middle-class medieval European woman's life.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>These travelers make ideal <strong>sourcing (HIPP) subjects</strong>: always consider each author's point of view (Ibn Battuta's Muslim lens, Marco Polo's outsider awe).</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Neo-Confucianism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Spread of Islam</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Papermaking</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Ibn Battuta</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Marco Polo</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Margery Kempe</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Gunpowder</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Champa rice</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.6","title":"Environmental Consequences of Connectivity","body":"<p>Trade moved <strong>crops</strong> as well as goods, with dramatic demographic effects. Fast-ripening <strong>Champa rice</strong> spread to China, driving <strong>population growth</strong>, new farming techniques, migration, and <strong>urbanization</strong>. <strong>Bananas</strong> spread to Sub-Saharan Africa, boosting population there, while growing demand for <strong>sugar and citrus</strong> foreshadowed the later use of enslaved labor on tropical plantations.</p>\n<p>The most devastating consequence was <strong>disease</strong>. The <strong>bubonic plague (Black Death)</strong> traveled with infected fleas along trade and Mongol routes, killing perhaps a third of Europe's population, helping to end <strong>feudalism</strong>: and tens of millions across China and Asia. It spread less in South Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa, which had fewer connected trading ports.</p>\n<p>Finally, rising populations strained the land, producing <strong>overgrazing, deforestation, and soil erosion</strong> in many regions.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>This topic is the payoff of the whole unit: the same networks that spread wealth and ideas also spread <strong>crops that grew populations</strong> and <strong>disease that collapsed them</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Champa rice</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Bananas</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Bubonic plague</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Black Death</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Urbanization</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Sugar</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Deforestation</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Decline of feudalism</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 2.7","title":"Comparing Networks of Exchange","body":"<p>Topic 2.7 asks you to <strong>compare</strong> the <strong>Silk Roads</strong>, the <strong>Indian Ocean</strong>, and the <strong>trans-Saharan</strong> routes. All three grew between 1200 and 1450, all three spread <strong>religions, technologies, and diseases</strong> along with goods, and all three created wealthy <strong>trading cities</strong>: Samarkand and Kashgar, Kilwa and Malacca, Timbuktu and Djenné.</p>\n<p>They differed in <strong>what</strong> they carried and <strong>how</strong>. Overland caravans on the Silk Roads mostly moved <strong>luxury goods</strong> such as silk and porcelain, because land transport was slow and costly. Indian Ocean ships, guided by the <strong>monsoon winds</strong>, carried larger volumes, including <strong>bulk goods</strong> such as rice, timber, and cotton. Trans-Saharan trade depended on the <strong>camel</strong>, the camel saddle, and oasis towns, and centered on <strong>gold and salt</strong>.</p>\n<p>States shaped every network. The <strong>Mongols</strong> protected the Silk Roads, <strong>Mali</strong> taxed and secured caravans, and the <strong>Sultanate of Malacca</strong> policed its strait. In each case, protecting trade brought rulers wealth and power.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-callout\"><span class=\"sg-callout-lbl\">AP Angle</span>A strong comparison names a <strong>similarity</strong> (all three spread Islam or Buddhism through merchants) and a <strong>difference</strong> (Indian Ocean trade moved bulk goods by sea; Silk Road trade moved luxuries by land) and explains <em>why</em>.</div>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\"><span class=\"sg-kw\">Luxury vs. bulk goods</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Monsoon winds</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Camel saddle</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Caravanserai</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Diasporic communities</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Trading cities</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Pax Mongolica</span></div>"}
],
3: [
  {"num":"Topic 3.1","title":"Empires Expand","body":"<p>Between 1450 and 1750, several large <strong>land-based empires</strong> expanded dramatically by using <strong>gunpowder weapons</strong>, cannons and muskets: to conquer rivals and centralize power. Historians call the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal states the <strong>gunpowder empires</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>Ottoman Empire</strong> (Sunni Muslim) captured <strong>Constantinople in 1453</strong> using massive siege cannons, ending the Byzantine Empire and seizing key trade routes. The <strong>Safavid Empire</strong> in Persia adopted <strong>Shia Islam</strong>, creating a lasting rivalry with its Sunni neighbors seen at the <strong>Battle of Chaldiran (1514)</strong>. The <strong>Mughal Empire</strong>, founded by Babur, ruled a Hindu-majority population in South Asia.</p><p>In East Asia the <strong>Ming</strong> and then the <strong>Manchu (Qing)</strong> dynasties governed China; the Qing were a non-Han people who conquered China in 1644 and expanded its borders. In West Africa the <strong>Songhai Empire</strong> grew wealthy controlling trans-Saharan trade.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Gunpowder Empires</span><span class='sg-kw'>Ottoman</span><span class='sg-kw'>Safavid</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mughal</span><span class='sg-kw'>Qing</span><span class='sg-kw'>Constantinople 1453</span><span class='sg-kw'>Chaldiran</span><span class='sg-kw'>Songhai</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 3.2","title":"Administration of Empires","body":"<p>Ruling a large empire required a sophisticated <strong>bureaucracy</strong> and reliable ways to raise revenue and command loyalty from diverse populations.</p><p>The Ottomans used the <strong>devshirme</strong>, taking Christian boys from the Balkans, converting them to Islam, and training them as administrators or elite <strong>Janissary</strong> soldiers loyal directly to the sultan. The Mughals relied on <strong>zamindars</strong>, local landowners who collected taxes. Many empires used <strong>tax farming</strong>, selling the right to collect taxes to private individuals.</p><p>China maintained a merit-based <strong>scholar-gentry bureaucracy</strong> chosen by civil-service exam. In Tokugawa Japan, <strong>samurai</strong> became salaried bureaucrats during a long era of peace. China also used the <strong>tribute system</strong> to manage relations with neighboring states.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Devshirme</span><span class='sg-kw'>Janissaries</span><span class='sg-kw'>Zamindars</span><span class='sg-kw'>Tax Farming</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bureaucracy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Scholar-Gentry</span><span class='sg-kw'>Salaried Samurai</span><span class='sg-kw'>Tribute System</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 3.3","title":"Belief Systems &amp; Legitimacy","body":"<p>Rulers legitimized their power through <strong>religion</strong>, monumental <strong>art and architecture</strong>, and claims of divine favor.</p><p>European monarchs claimed the <strong>divine right of kings</strong>, that their authority came from God. Muslim empires drew legitimacy from Islam, though the <strong>Sunni-Shia</strong> split divided them. The Mughal emperor <strong>Akbar</strong> promoted religious tolerance, abolishing the <strong>jizya</strong> tax on non-Muslims and creating a syncretic faith. His descendant <strong>Aurangzeb</strong> reversed this, reimposing the jizya and persecuting non-Muslims, which weakened the empire. The syncretic religion of <strong>Sikhism</strong> also emerged in this environment.</p><p>In Europe the <strong>Protestant Reformation</strong>, begun by Martin Luther, split Western Christianity and prompted the Catholic <strong>Counter-Reformation</strong>. Rulers everywhere built monuments: the <strong>Taj Mahal</strong>, <strong>Versailles</strong>, the <strong>Suleymaniye Mosque</strong>, to display wealth and legitimacy.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Divine Right</span><span class='sg-kw'>Sunni-Shia</span><span class='sg-kw'>Akbar</span><span class='sg-kw'>Jizya</span><span class='sg-kw'>Aurangzeb</span><span class='sg-kw'>Sikhism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Reformation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Counter-Reformation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Taj Mahal</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 3.4","title":"Comparison in Land-Based Empires","body":"<p>The AP skill of <strong>comparison</strong> asks you to analyze similarities and differences in how these empires expanded and governed.</p><p>All of the land-based empires <strong>centralized power</strong>, but they used different tools. They legitimized rule through <strong>religion</strong> (Islam, Christianity, the Mandate of Heaven), <strong>art and architecture</strong>, and elaborate <strong>bureaucracies</strong>, and they funded their states through <strong>tax systems</strong> such as tax farming, zamindars, and tribute.</p><p>A key difference lay in <strong>religious policy</strong>: some rulers (Akbar) embraced tolerance to govern diverse subjects, while others (Aurangzeb, or Spain's Catholic monarchs) enforced conformity. Comparing these choices reveals how rulers balanced the needs of diverse populations against the desire for unity.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Centralization</span><span class='sg-kw'>Legitimacy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Comparison</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bureaucracy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Religious Policy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Absolutism</span></div>"}
],
4: [
  {"num":"Topic 4.1","title":"Technological Innovations","body":"<p>Long-distance ocean travel became possible thanks to a blend of borrowed and improved <strong>maritime technologies</strong>.</p><p>The Portuguese <strong>caravel</strong> was a small, maneuverable ship; the <strong>lateen sail</strong> (from the Indian Ocean world) let ships sail against the wind. The <strong>magnetic compass</strong> (from China) and the <strong>astrolabe</strong> (refined in the Muslim world) allowed sailors to navigate the open ocean. Knowledge of <strong>monsoon winds</strong> and ocean currents made planned voyages possible.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Caravel</span><span class='sg-kw'>Lateen Sail</span><span class='sg-kw'>Astrolabe</span><span class='sg-kw'>Magnetic Compass</span><span class='sg-kw'>Monsoon Winds</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.2","title":"Exploration: Causes &amp; Events","body":"<p>Motivated by <strong>God, gold, and glory</strong>, European states sponsored voyages of exploration across the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian Oceans.</p><p>Portugal, encouraged by figures like Prince Henry, pushed down the African coast toward Asian spice markets. Sailing for Spain, <strong>Christopher Columbus</strong> reached the Americas in <strong>1492</strong>, and <strong>Ferdinand Magellan's</strong> expedition first circumnavigated the globe. To avoid conflict, Spain and Portugal divided the non-European world in the <strong>Treaty of Tordesillas (1494)</strong>. States funded these risky voyages hoping for wealth, converts, and prestige.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Columbus</span><span class='sg-kw'>Magellan</span><span class='sg-kw'>Treaty of Tordesillas</span><span class='sg-kw'>God Gold Glory</span><span class='sg-kw'>Prince Henry</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.3","title":"The Columbian Exchange","body":"<p>The <strong>Columbian Exchange</strong> was the transfer of plants, animals, people, and diseases between the Eastern and Western Hemispheres after 1492, one of the most transformative events in world history.</p><p>Old World <strong>diseases</strong>, especially <strong>smallpox</strong>, devastated Indigenous American populations who had no immunity, causing catastrophic population collapse. New World <strong>crops</strong> like maize, potatoes, and cassava spread to Afro-Eurasia and fueled population growth. Old World animals (horses, cattle, pigs) and <strong>cash crops</strong> like sugar reshaped economies and drove demand for coerced labor.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Columbian Exchange</span><span class='sg-kw'>Smallpox</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cash Crops</span><span class='sg-kw'>New World Crops</span><span class='sg-kw'>Population Collapse</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.4","title":"Maritime Empires Established","body":"<p>Europeans built <strong>maritime empires</strong> based on controlling sea lanes and extracting wealth.</p><p>Portugal created a <strong>trading-post empire</strong>, seizing fortified ports like <strong>Goa and Malacca</strong> to tax Indian Ocean commerce rather than conquering large territories. Spain conquered the <strong>Aztec and Inca</strong> and built a land empire worked by coerced labor: the <strong>encomienda</strong> granted colonists the right to native labor, and the <strong>mit'a</strong> forced labor in silver mines like <strong>Potosi</strong>. To meet plantation labor demand, Europeans expanded the <strong>Atlantic slave trade</strong>, forcing millions of Africans across the <strong>Middle Passage</strong>.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Trading Post Empire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Encomienda</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mit'a</span><span class='sg-kw'>Atlantic Slave Trade</span><span class='sg-kw'>Middle Passage</span><span class='sg-kw'>Potosi</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.5","title":"Maritime Empires Maintained","body":"<p>States and merchants developed new economic tools to sustain and profit from global empires.</p><p><strong>Mercantilism</strong> held that colonies existed to enrich the mother country by supplying raw materials and buying finished goods. <strong>Joint-stock companies</strong> like the Dutch <strong>VOC</strong> and the British <strong>East India Company</strong> pooled investors' money to fund and control trade, spreading risk. American <strong>silver</strong>: much of it flowing to <strong>Ming China</strong>, helped create the first truly global economy, linked by the <strong>triangular trade</strong> across the Atlantic.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Mercantilism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Joint-Stock Company</span><span class='sg-kw'>VOC</span><span class='sg-kw'>Silver Trade</span><span class='sg-kw'>Triangular Trade</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.6","title":"Challenges to State Power","body":"<p>Expanding states faced <strong>resistance</strong> from within and without.</p><p>Enslaved people resisted through revolts and by forming <strong>maroon communities</strong> of escapees. Indigenous peoples fought back, as in the <strong>Pueblo Revolt</strong> in North America. In Africa, <strong>Queen Nzinga</strong> of Ndongo and Matamba resisted Portuguese expansion for decades. In Europe, nobles challenged rising royal power, as in the French <strong>Fronde</strong>. Empire-building was contested at every step.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Maroon Communities</span><span class='sg-kw'>Pueblo Revolt</span><span class='sg-kw'>Queen Nzinga</span><span class='sg-kw'>Fronde</span><span class='sg-kw'>Resistance</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.7","title":"Changing Social Hierarchies","body":"<p>Global connections produced new <strong>social hierarchies</strong> and blended cultures.</p><p>In Spanish America the <strong>casta system</strong> ranked people by ancestry: <strong>peninsulares</strong> (Spanish-born) at the top, then <strong>creoles</strong>, <strong>mestizos</strong> (mixed European-Indigenous), and enslaved Africans and Indigenous people at the bottom. The mixing of peoples also created <strong>syncretic religions</strong> like <strong>Vodun</strong> and Santeria, blending African and Christian beliefs. Existing elites in Asia and Africa sometimes gained or lost status through new trade wealth.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Casta System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Peninsulares</span><span class='sg-kw'>Creoles</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mestizo</span><span class='sg-kw'>Syncretic Religions</span><span class='sg-kw'>Vodun</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 4.8","title":"Continuity &amp; Change","body":"<p>The AP skill of <strong>continuity and change</strong> asks how this era both transformed the world and echoed older patterns.</p><p>The biggest <strong>change</strong> was the creation of a single, interconnected <strong>global economy</strong> linking the Americas, Europe, Africa, and Asia for the first time. Yet important <strong>continuities</strong> remained: coerced labor, powerful merchant networks, and the wealth of Asian economies (India and China) that still dominated global manufacturing. Recognizing both sides shows why 1450&ndash;1750 was a turning point without being a total break from the past.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Global Economy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Continuity and Change</span><span class='sg-kw'>Coerced Labor</span><span class='sg-kw'>Interconnection</span></div>"}
],
5: [
  {"num":"Topic 5.1","title":"The Enlightenment","body":"<p>The <strong>Enlightenment</strong> was an eighteenth-century intellectual movement that applied reason and empirical observation to human affairs, politics, economics, religion, and society. Thinkers such as <strong>John Locke</strong> argued that governments derive authority from the consent of the governed and that individuals possess natural rights to life, liberty, and property. <strong>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</strong> developed the idea of the <strong>social contract</strong>: legitimate government rests on popular sovereignty. <strong>Montesquieu</strong> called for separation of powers to prevent tyranny.</p><p>Enlightenment ideas spread through <em>salons</em>, coffeehouses, pamphlets, and the <em>Encyclopédie</em>, and they did not stay confined to Europe. American colonists used Locke’s natural rights theory to justify independence in 1776. French revolutionaries declared ‘Liberty, Equality, Fraternity’ in 1789. Enslaved people in Saint-Domingue (Haiti) used French revolutionary language to demand freedom, launching the only successful slave revolution in history in 1791. Creole elites across Latin America invoked Enlightenment principles against Spanish mercantilist control.</p><p>Crucially, the Enlightenment was not universally applied. Most Enlightenment thinkers did not advocate for the rights of women, enslaved people, or colonized peoples, creating contradictions that reformers and revolutionaries would exploit throughout the nineteenth century.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Enlightenment</span><span class='sg-kw'>John Locke</span><span class='sg-kw'>Natural Rights</span><span class='sg-kw'>Social Contract</span><span class='sg-kw'>Montesquieu</span><span class='sg-kw'>Popular Sovereignty</span><span class='sg-kw'>Voltaire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Rousseau</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.2","title":"Nationalism and Revolution","body":"<p>The period 1750–1900 witnessed a wave of <strong>Atlantic Revolutions</strong> driven by Enlightenment ideals, colonial resentment, and economic grievances. The <strong>American Revolution (1776)</strong> produced the first nation-state founded explicitly on Enlightenment principles. The <strong>French Revolution (1789)</strong> dismantled the Old Regime, but also unleashed the Terror and Napoleon’s wars, spreading revolutionary ideas across Europe and the Americas.</p><p>The <strong>Haitian Revolution (1791–1804)</strong> was the most radical Atlantic Revolution. Enslaved Africans on the French colony of Saint-Domingue, inspired by the Declaration of the Rights of Man, rose in rebellion. Under <strong>Toussaint L’Ouverture</strong> and later Jean-Jacques Dessalines, they defeated French, British, and Spanish forces, abolishing slavery and establishing <strong>Haiti (1804)</strong> as the first Black republic and second independent nation in the Americas.</p><p>Latin American independence movements (1810s–1820s) were led by creole elites who resented Spanish mercantilist restrictions. <strong>Simón Bolívar</strong> (Venezuela, Colombia, Peru) and <strong>José de San Martín</strong> (Argentina, Chile) used both Enlightenment ideas and military force to win independence. By 1825, most of Spanish America was independent, though inequality remained.</p><p><strong>Nationalism</strong>: the idea that peoples sharing a common language, history, or culture should form their own states, also reshaped Europe. Germany and Italy unified as nation-states in 1871. Nationalist movements challenged the multi-ethnic Ottoman and Habsburg Empires throughout the nineteenth century.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>American Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>French Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Haitian Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Toussaint L’Ouverture</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bolivar</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nationalism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Latin American Independence</span><span class='sg-kw'>Atlantic Revolutions</span><span class='sg-kw'>Self-Determination</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.3","title":"Industrial Revolution Begins","body":"<p>The <strong>Industrial Revolution</strong> began in Britain in the 1760s–1780s, transforming production from hand tools and cottage industry to machine manufacturing in centralized factories. Britain’s advantages included abundant <strong>coal and iron</strong>, a network of rivers and canals, a colonial empire providing raw materials and markets, a culture of practical invention, and relatively stable property rights that encouraged investment.</p><p>Key innovations: the <strong>spinning jenny</strong> (Hargreaves, 1764) and <strong>water frame</strong> (Arkwright, 1769) mechanized textile production. James Watt’s improved <strong>steam engine (1769)</strong> provided portable power that could drive machines anywhere. Steam-powered textile mills, mines, and factories transformed Britain’s economy and landscape.</p><p>The social consequences were profound. Workers moved from countryside to industrial cities like Manchester, Birmingham, and Leeds, <strong>rapid urbanization</strong> created crowded, unsanitary conditions. Women and children worked long hours in factories and mines. A new <strong>working class (proletariat)</strong> and middle class (<em>bourgeoisie</em>) emerged, displacing the traditional rural gentry as the dominant social forces.</p><p>Britain’s early industrialization created the ‘workshop of the world’, by 1850 it produced more iron and coal than the rest of the world combined. But this dominance was temporary: by 1900, the US and Germany had surpassed Britain in industrial output.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Industrial Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Steam Engine</span><span class='sg-kw'>James Watt</span><span class='sg-kw'>Spinning Jenny</span><span class='sg-kw'>Factory System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Urbanization</span><span class='sg-kw'>Proletariat</span><span class='sg-kw'>Coal &amp; Iron</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.4","title":"Industrialization Spreads","body":"<p>By the mid-to-late nineteenth century, industrialization spread beyond Britain to Western Europe, the United States, and parts of Asia. The <strong>second industrial revolution</strong> (c. 1870–1914) was characterized by steel, chemicals, electricity, and petroleum, newer industries requiring scientific knowledge and larger capital investment.</p><p><strong>Germany</strong> emerged as the industrial giant of the late nineteenth century, overtaking Britain by 1900. Germany’s chemical and steel industries, Krupp, Bayer, BASF, dominated world markets. The <strong>United States</strong> industrialized rapidly after the Civil War, its vast internal market and resources making it the world’s largest economy by 1900.</p><p>In Asia, the <strong>Meiji Restoration (1868)</strong> transformed Japan from a feudal society to an industrial power in a single generation. Meiji leaders sent students abroad, hired Western advisors, built railroads, established state-owned factories, and reformed education. By 1905, Japan’s industrial strength enabled it to defeat Russia in the Russo-Japanese War, the first Asian power to defeat a European one in modern times.</p><p>Other states attempted to industrialize or modernize to resist European pressure. The Qing Empire launched the <strong>Self-Strengthening Movement (1861–1895)</strong> to adopt Western technology while preserving Confucian values. The Ottoman Empire initiated the <strong>Tanzimat reforms (1839–1876)</strong> to modernize administration and the military. Both efforts had limited success against the full force of European industrial capitalism.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Second Industrial Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>German Industrialization</span><span class='sg-kw'>Meiji Restoration</span><span class='sg-kw'>Self-Strengthening Movement</span><span class='sg-kw'>Tanzimat Reforms</span><span class='sg-kw'>Steel</span><span class='sg-kw'>Electricity</span><span class='sg-kw'>Railroad Networks</span><span class='sg-kw'>US Industrialization</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.5","title":"Technology of the Industrial Age","body":"<p>Industrialization produced not just factories but a revolution in transportation, communication, and finance that knit the world together. The <strong>railroad</strong>: made practical by steam engines, dramatically reduced the cost and time of overland travel. By 1870 Britain had 15,000 miles of track; the US Transcontinental Railroad (1869) linked the coasts; railroads opened Siberia, India, and the interior of Africa to resource extraction and settlement.</p><p>The <strong>telegraph</strong> (1837, Samuel Morse) enabled near-instantaneous communication across continents. The first transatlantic telegraph cable (1866) linked Britain and America, allowing commodity prices and financial news to travel faster than any ship. The <strong>steamship</strong> replaced sail on major ocean routes, making voyages faster, cheaper, and more predictable.</p><p>The <strong>Suez Canal (1869)</strong> cut the distance from Europe to Asia by thousands of miles. These transportation and communication innovations created truly global markets for the first time, allowing cotton grown in Egypt to be manufactured into cloth in Manchester and sold in South America within weeks.</p><p>Financial innovations also accelerated industrialization: <strong>limited liability corporations</strong> allowed investors to pool capital without risking personal bankruptcy; <strong>stock exchanges</strong> in London, Paris, and New York mobilized the savings of thousands into industrial investment; <strong>commercial banks</strong> provided loans and credit to fund expansion.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Railroad</span><span class='sg-kw'>Telegraph</span><span class='sg-kw'>Steamship</span><span class='sg-kw'>Suez Canal</span><span class='sg-kw'>Limited Liability</span><span class='sg-kw'>Stock Exchange</span><span class='sg-kw'>Commercial Banks</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.6","title":"Industrialization and Capitalism","body":"<p>Industrialization was driven by <strong>capitalism</strong>, the economic system based on private ownership of the means of production, market competition, and profit-seeking investment. The dominant economic ideology of the era was <strong>laissez-faire</strong> (French: ‘let it be’), developed by <strong>Adam Smith</strong> in <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776): free markets, not government intervention, produce the most efficient allocation of resources and the greatest overall wealth.</p><p>Laissez-faire economists criticized mercantilism (government-directed trade and monopolies) and argued for free trade. Britain repealed its Corn Laws (1846) and championed free trade globally, partly because British industry was competitive enough to benefit from open markets.</p><p>However, in practice, industrial capitalism often operated through government-chartered companies and state power. The <strong>East India Company</strong> governed much of India; the <strong>British South Africa Company</strong> administered vast territories in southern Africa. These chartered monopolies combined corporate profit-seeking with territorial control, making them instruments of both imperialism and capitalism simultaneously.</p><p>The era also saw the rise of <strong>transnational corporations</strong> and global financial networks. European banks invested in railroads, mines, and plantations across Asia, Africa, and Latin America, extracting profits while shaping the development of local economies to serve European industrial needs.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Capitalism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Adam Smith</span><span class='sg-kw'>Laissez-Faire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Free Trade</span><span class='sg-kw'>East India Company</span><span class='sg-kw'>Chartered Companies</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mercantilism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Transnational Corporations</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.7","title":"Economic Developments and Innovations","body":"<p>The Industrial Revolution created a globally integrated economy for the first time. Raw materials: cotton from Egypt and the American South, rubber from Congo and Brazil, tin from Malaysia, tea from India and China, flowed to European factories, while manufactured goods flowed back to colonial and semi-colonial markets worldwide.</p><p>The period saw major innovations in <strong>banking and finance</strong>. The <strong>gold standard</strong> (fully established c. 1870s) pegged currencies to gold, creating exchange rate stability that facilitated international trade and investment. <strong>Central banks</strong> managed national money supplies. <strong>Insurance companies</strong> reduced the risk of commercial ventures. These innovations lowered the cost of doing business globally and enabled the massive capital flows that financed industrialization worldwide.</p><p><strong>New forms of business organization</strong> emerged. <strong>Corporations with limited liability</strong> (legally established in Britain 1856) allowed entrepreneurs to raise capital from many investors without each risking personal ruin. Large industrial corporations: Krupp, Standard Oil, De Beers, became more powerful than many governments.</p><p>The integration of global markets created both winners and losers. British textile exports devastated Indian textile production; European manufactured goods undercut artisan industries from Mexico to Persia to China. Regions with resources but little industrial capacity were incorporated into the global economy as raw material suppliers, a pattern that shaped development disparities for the next century.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Gold Standard</span><span class='sg-kw'>Central Banks</span><span class='sg-kw'>Limited Liability Corporation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Global Markets</span><span class='sg-kw'>Raw Material Export</span><span class='sg-kw'>Deindustrialization</span><span class='sg-kw'>Capital Investment</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.8","title":"Reactions to the Industrial Economy","body":"<p>The inequalities, dangerous conditions, and class divisions created by industrial capitalism provoked powerful opposition movements. <strong>Karl Marx</strong> and <strong>Friedrich Engels</strong>, in the <em>Communist Manifesto</em> (1848) and <em>Das Kapital</em> (1867), argued that capitalism inevitably exploited workers (the <em>proletariat</em>) for the benefit of capitalist owners (the <em>bourgeoisie</em>). They predicted a workers’ revolution would overthrow capitalism and establish a classless, communist society.</p><p><strong>Socialism</strong> took many forms. <strong>Utopian socialists</strong> (Robert Owen, Charles Fourier) tried to build model communities. <strong>Marxist socialists</strong> organized political parties. <strong>Anarchists</strong> (Mikhail Bakunin) rejected all government authority. <strong>Social Democrats</strong> worked within democratic systems to achieve gradual reform. The <strong>First International (1864)</strong> and <strong>Second International (1889)</strong> attempted to coordinate workers’ movements across national borders.</p><p><strong>Labor unions</strong> and trade unions organized workers to strike for better wages, shorter hours, and safer conditions. The <strong>eight-hour workday</strong> movement, banning of child labor, factory safety regulations, and social insurance programs were all won through labor organizing and political pressure.</p><p>Conservative governments responded with both repression and reform. <strong>Bismarck</strong>’s Germany introduced the world’s first social insurance programs (1880s): accident insurance, old-age pensions, explicitly to undercut socialist political organizing.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Karl Marx</span><span class='sg-kw'>Communist Manifesto</span><span class='sg-kw'>Socialism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Labor Unions</span><span class='sg-kw'>Proletariat</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bourgeoisie</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bismarck’s Reforms</span><span class='sg-kw'>Anarchism</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.9","title":"Society and the Industrial Age","body":"<p>Industrialization reshaped who people were and how they lived. A new <strong>industrial middle class</strong> of factory owners, merchants, and professionals gained wealth and political power; in Britain the <strong>Reform Act of 1832</strong> gave many middle-class men the vote. A much larger <strong>working class</strong> labored long hours in factories and mines for low wages.</p><p>Cities grew faster than housing and sanitation. <strong>Urbanization</strong> produced crowded slums like those Friedrich Engels described in Manchester, along with epidemics such as <strong>cholera</strong>, which led to public health reforms like sewers and clean water. <strong>Child labor</strong> was common until reforms such as Britain's <strong>Factory Act of 1833</strong> began to limit it.</p><p>Gender roles changed by class. The middle class embraced the ideal of <strong>separate spheres</strong> (the cult of domesticity), while many working-class women earned wages in textile mills or as servants. These pressures fed the reform and labor movements of Topic 5.8.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Middle Class</span><span class='sg-kw'>Working Class</span><span class='sg-kw'>Urbanization</span><span class='sg-kw'>Tenements</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cult of Domesticity</span><span class='sg-kw'>Child Labor</span><span class='sg-kw'>Factory Act of 1833</span><span class='sg-kw'>Reform Act of 1832</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 5.10","title":"Continuity and Change in the Industrial Age","body":"<p>Between 1750 and 1900, production shifted from homes and workshops (the <strong>putting-out system</strong>) to <strong>factories</strong> powered by steam. Western Europe, the United States, and later Japan industrialized, while the share of world manufacturing held by China and India fell sharply, a shift called the <strong>Great Divergence</strong>. Atlantic revolutions spread ideas of natural rights, nationalism, and constitutional government.</p><p>Much also stayed the same. Most people in Asia, Africa, and Latin America still farmed. <strong>Coerced labor</strong> continued in new forms: slavery was abolished (Britain 1833, Brazil 1888), but plantations turned to <strong>indentured labor</strong>. Some states, such as tsarist <strong>Russia</strong>, kept absolute monarchy while making limited reforms like the emancipation of the serfs in 1861.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Putting-Out System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Factory System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Great Divergence</span><span class='sg-kw'>Indentured Labor</span><span class='sg-kw'>Emancipation of the Serfs</span><span class='sg-kw'>Continuity</span><span class='sg-kw'>Change</span></div>"}
],
6: [
  {"num":"Topic 6.1","title":"Rationales for Imperialism","body":"<p>European states, the United States, and Japan justified imperial expansion through a cluster of overlapping ideologies that combined economic interest with racial and moral arguments.</p><p><strong>Social Darwinism</strong> misapplied Darwin's biological theory of natural selection to human societies. Herbert Spencer's 'survival of the fittest' framed imperial conquest as the natural triumph of superior civilizations over inferior ones. This ideology gave a pseudoscientific veneer to racial hierarchy, claiming European dominance was biologically inevitable rather than historically contingent.</p><p><strong>Scientific racism</strong> reinforced Social Darwinism with claims about racial biology, skull measurements, intelligence rankings, and hierarchies of civilization that placed Europeans at the top and Africans and Asians at the bottom. These claims were used in everything from school textbooks to parliamentary debates to justify conquest.</p><p>The <strong>Civilizing Mission</strong> (France: <em>Mission civilisatrice</em>) argued that European powers had a moral <em>duty</em> to bring Christianity, Western education, medicine, and 'civilization' to 'backward' peoples. Rudyard Kipling's 1899 poem 'The White Man's Burden' encapsulated this paternalistic ideology: colonialism as an act of sacrifice and service.</p><p>Economic arguments were equally central. <strong>Jules Ferry</strong>, French Prime Minister, explicitly linked imperialism to industrial policy: European factories needed raw materials, overseas markets for surplus goods, and outlets for surplus capital investment. Colonies solved all three problems simultaneously.</p><p>Finally, <strong>strategic and nationalist rivalry</strong> drove imperial competition: if Britain claimed a territory, France felt compelled to claim its neighbor. The Scramble for Africa accelerated precisely because no power wanted rivals to gain an advantage.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Social Darwinism</span><span class='sg-kw'>White Man's Burden</span><span class='sg-kw'>Civilizing Mission</span><span class='sg-kw'>Scientific Racism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Jules Ferry</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cecil Rhodes</span><span class='sg-kw'>Kipling</span><span class='sg-kw'>Herbert Spencer</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.2","title":"State Expansion","body":"<p>Between 1870 and 1914, European powers, the United States, and Japan dramatically expanded their territorial control, claiming approximately 20% of the Earth's land surface in just four decades.</p><p>The <strong>Scramble for Africa</strong> (c. 1880–1914) was the most dramatic expression of New Imperialism. At the <strong>Berlin Conference (1884–85)</strong>, 14 European powers met, without a single African representative, to establish rules for claiming African territory. The principle of 'effective occupation' required colonial powers to demonstrate actual governance to legitimize claims. By 1914, only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent.</p><p>Colonial control took different forms. <strong>Direct rule</strong> replaced existing political structures with European administrators (French model in Algeria, Belgian Congo). <strong>Indirect rule</strong>: practiced extensively by Britain: maintained existing local leaders as intermediaries who governed on behalf of the colonial power. Both systems served European interests, but indirect rule was cheaper and created local collaborators.</p><p>British consolidation in <strong>India</strong> followed the Indian Rebellion of 1857, when the East India Company was replaced by direct Crown governance. Queen Victoria became Empress of India in 1877. India became the jewel of the British Empire, a supplier of cotton, tea, and soldiers.</p><p>The <strong>Meiji Restoration (1868)</strong> transformed Japan from a feudal state into an industrial and military power within a generation. Facing the threat of Western colonization, Japan deliberately modernized its military, built railroads and factories, and adopted selective Western institutions. By 1895, Japan had defeated China (taking Taiwan and Korea); by 1905, Japan defeated Russia, the first non-Western nation to defeat a European power in modern warfare.</p><p>The United States expanded across North America through treaties, purchases, and wars, then extended overseas: annexing Hawaii (1898), winning the Philippines, Guam, and Puerto Rico from Spain (1898), and establishing a protectorate over Cuba.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Berlin Conference</span><span class='sg-kw'>Scramble for Africa</span><span class='sg-kw'>Meiji Restoration</span><span class='sg-kw'>Direct Rule</span><span class='sg-kw'>Indirect Rule</span><span class='sg-kw'>Protectorate</span><span class='sg-kw'>Sphere of Influence</span><span class='sg-kw'>Extraterritoriality</span><span class='sg-kw'>Settler Colony</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.3","title":"Indigenous Responses to State Expansion","body":"<p>Colonized peoples were not passive victims. Responses to imperial expansion ranged from armed resistance and diplomatic negotiation to selective accommodation and cultural preservation.</p><p><strong>Armed resistance</strong> was widespread. The <strong>Indian Rebellion of 1857</strong> (often called the Sepoy Mutiny) began when Indian soldiers in the British East India Company's army refused to use rifle cartridges allegedly greased with pig and cow fat. The rebellion quickly spread to a broader uprising rooted in decades of accumulated grievances: land annexation, cultural suppression, and displacement of traditional elites. Though ultimately suppressed, it forced the British Crown to assume direct rule of India.</p><p>In Africa, the <strong>Zulu Wars (1879)</strong> produced the Battle of Isandlwana, the largest British defeat by an indigenous African force, before Britain ultimately prevailed. In 1896, Emperor <strong>Menelik II of Ethiopia</strong> decisively defeated Italy at the <strong>Battle of Adwa</strong>, the only complete African military victory against a European colonial army during the Scramble for Africa. Ethiopia remained independent.</p><p>The <strong>Boxer Rebellion (1900)</strong> in China targeted foreign missionaries, businesspeople, and Chinese Christians. When the Qing government allied with the Boxers and declared war on foreign powers, an eight-nation alliance invaded and suppressed the rebellion. The resulting <strong>Boxer Protocol</strong> imposed massive indemnities and deepened foreign control.</p><p><strong>Accommodation</strong> and <strong>collaboration</strong> were also common. Some local leaders worked within colonial systems, gaining privileges and power as intermediaries. Others pursued selective modernization: China's <strong>Self-Strengthening Movement</strong> attempted to adopt Western military technology while preserving Chinese political and cultural structures, summarized as 'Chinese learning for the essence; Western learning for practical use.'</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Indian Rebellion 1857</span><span class='sg-kw'>Battle of Adwa</span><span class='sg-kw'>Zulu Wars</span><span class='sg-kw'>Boxer Rebellion</span><span class='sg-kw'>Self-Strengthening Movement</span><span class='sg-kw'>Accommodation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Menelik II</span><span class='sg-kw'>Boxer Protocol</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.4","title":"Global Economic Development","body":"<p>Industrialization transformed not only European economies but global economic relationships, integrating the world into a single system on deeply unequal terms.</p><p><strong>Industrial capitalism</strong> created surplus production that required new markets and new sources of raw materials. European factories consumed cotton from Egypt and India, rubber from the Congo and Brazil, tea from Ceylon and China, and metals from Africa and Latin America. This demand restructured agriculture and labor worldwide.</p><p><strong>Railroad imperialism</strong> was the physical infrastructure of economic integration. By 1900, over 200,000 miles of track crisscrossed the globe. Railroads built in colonial territories were designed primarily to extract resources from interiors to coastal ports for export, not to connect domestic markets. This produced 'export enclave' economies structurally oriented toward the needs of distant industrial consumers, not local populations.</p><p><strong>Free trade imperialism</strong>: the British policy of forcing open foreign markets to British goods, created informal empires without formal annexation. Latin American nations nominally independent after the 1820s were economically dependent on British capital and markets: British banks financed their governments, British merchants dominated their ports, and British manufacturers supplied their consumer goods.</p><p><strong>Monoculture</strong>: the conversion of diverse agriculture to a single cash crop for export, created both short-term profit and long-term vulnerability. Egyptian cotton, Indian tea, Cuban sugar, and Guatemalan bananas all made colonial territories dependent on global commodity prices they had no power to influence.</p><p>The result was a global economic order that generated enormous wealth for industrial nations while limiting development in colonial territories, a structural inequality that persisted long after formal independence.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Industrial Capitalism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Railroad Imperialism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Free Trade Imperialism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Monoculture</span><span class='sg-kw'>Commodity Crops</span><span class='sg-kw'>Export Enclave Economy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Laissez-Faire</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.5","title":"Economic Imperialism","body":"<p>Economic imperialism involved the use of economic pressure, debt, and unequal treaties to control other nations without formal annexation, what historians call <strong>informal empire</strong>.</p><p>China provides the clearest case. The <strong>First Opium War (1839–42)</strong> began when China's Commissioner <strong>Lin Zexu</strong> destroyed 20,000 chests of British opium, a moral stand against a trade destroying Chinese society. Britain responded militarily, and China's defeat produced the <strong>Treaty of Nanjing (1842)</strong>: the first of the 'unequal treaties.' China ceded Hong Kong, opened five treaty ports, granted <strong>extraterritoriality</strong> (British citizens immune from Chinese law in China), and paid a massive indemnity.</p><p>The <strong>Most-Favored Nation clause</strong> ensured that any concession China granted to one Western power automatically extended to all. The Second Opium War (1856–60) produced further concessions. By 1900, Western powers had carved China into <strong>spheres of influence</strong>, zones of exclusive economic privilege, while the Qing government remained nominally sovereign.</p><p><strong>Gunboat diplomacy</strong>: the use or threat of naval force to compel weaker states to accept economic terms, was practiced by Britain throughout the 19th century, not only in China but in Latin America and the Ottoman Empire.</p><p>In Latin America, formally independent nations after the 1820s remained economically dependent on Britain and, later, the United States, providing the raw material of what would later be called <strong>neocolonialism</strong>: control through economic dominance rather than direct political rule.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Opium Wars</span><span class='sg-kw'>Treaty of Nanjing</span><span class='sg-kw'>Extraterritoriality</span><span class='sg-kw'>Most-Favored Nation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Unequal Treaties</span><span class='sg-kw'>Gunboat Diplomacy</span><span class='sg-kw'>Informal Empire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Lin Zexu</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.6","title":"Causes of Migration in an Industrializing World","body":"<p>The nineteenth century witnessed one of history's largest mass migrations, as tens of millions of people moved within and across continents in search of economic opportunity, safety, or simply survival.</p><p>Historians use the <strong>push-pull model</strong> to analyze migration. <strong>Push factors</strong> drove people out of their home regions: famine (the Irish Potato Famine, 1845–52, killed ~1 million and drove ~2 million to emigrate), poverty, lack of land, political persecution, and violence. <strong>Pull factors</strong> attracted migrants to destinations: available land, labor demand, higher wages, and political freedom.</p><p>Industrial expansion in the United States, Argentina, Australia, and Canada created enormous demand for unskilled labor, particularly for dangerous infrastructure work that local populations avoided. Chinese workers built the western half of the American transcontinental railroad (1863–69); Irish immigrants built the eastern half. Indian workers labored in South African mines and Caribbean sugar fields.</p><p>The abolition of the Atlantic slave trade created labor shortages on plantations, replaced by <strong>indentured servitude</strong>: contract labor binding migrants (primarily from India and China) to 5-year terms. Though technically voluntary, the system was often coercive, workers were deceived about conditions, could not break contracts, and faced criminal penalties for non-compliance. Over 1.5 million Indians migrated as indentured laborers between 1834 and 1920.</p><p><strong>Chain migration</strong>: where established migrants helped family and community members emigrate, created concentrated ethnic communities in destination cities, becoming self-sustaining networks that channeled future migration from the same origin regions.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Push Factors</span><span class='sg-kw'>Pull Factors</span><span class='sg-kw'>Indentured Servitude</span><span class='sg-kw'>Coolie Labor</span><span class='sg-kw'>Chain Migration</span><span class='sg-kw'>Irish Famine</span><span class='sg-kw'>Transcontinental Railroad</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.7","title":"Effects of Migration","body":"<p>Mass migration transformed both sending and receiving societies, creating new cultural communities, provoking nativist reactions, and reshaping economies on both ends of migration flows.</p><p><strong>Diaspora communities</strong>: groups living outside their ancestral homeland while maintaining cultural ties to it, emerged across the globe. Chinese communities in San Francisco, Southeast Asia, and the Caribbean; Indian communities in South Africa, Fiji, Trinidad, and Mauritius; Irish communities in Boston, New York, and Liverpool all preserved languages, religions, foods, and cultural practices across generations while also adapting to new environments.</p><p><strong>Cultural syncretism</strong>: the blending of cultural elements, produced new hybrid forms: Caribbean Creole languages, Chinese-Jamaican food, the fusion religions of Indo-Caribbean Hinduism. Diaspora communities were neither fully the culture of their origin nor fully assimilated into the receiving society.</p><p>Migration also provoked powerful <strong>nativist</strong> responses. The <strong>Chinese Exclusion Act (1882)</strong> was the first U.S. federal law restricting immigration by race and nationality, passed in response to anti-Chinese sentiment driven by economic competition and racial prejudice. Similar restrictions followed in Australia (White Australia Policy, 1901), Canada, and New Zealand. These laws reflected how receiving societies struggled with the cultural and economic changes that mass migration brought.</p><p><strong>Remittances</strong>: money sent by migrants back to families in origin countries, represented a significant economic flow. Italian migrants in Argentina, Chinese workers in California, and Indian laborers in Trinidad all sent money home, partially offsetting the human cost of emigration and funding development in sending regions.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Chinese Exclusion Act</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nativism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Diaspora</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cultural Syncretism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Remittances</span><span class='sg-kw'>Chinatown</span><span class='sg-kw'>White Australia Policy</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 6.8","title":"Causation in the Imperial Age","body":"<p>The AP skill of causation requires you to explain not just what happened but why, and to distinguish between necessary conditions, triggering causes, and underlying structural forces.</p><p>The central causal argument for Unit 6: <strong>industrialization caused the New Imperialism</strong>. Industrial production created three interconnected needs that colonial expansion satisfied: (1) raw material supplies that domestic territories could not provide at sufficient scale; (2) overseas markets for surplus manufactured goods; (3) investment opportunities for the surplus capital that industrial profits generated. Historian J.A. Hobson made this argument in <em>Imperialism: A Study</em> (1902); Lenin extended it in <em>Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</em> (1916).</p><p>But causation was not purely economic. <strong>Nationalist rivalry</strong> among European powers accelerated imperial competition: Germany's unification (1871) created a new industrial power seeking colonial prestige; France sought to recover national dignity after defeat in the Franco-Prussian War through overseas empire. The <strong>technological gap</strong>: especially the Maxim machine gun, quinine (protecting against malaria), and steam-powered gunboats, made conquest militarily feasible in ways it had not been earlier.</p><p>For the <strong>continuity-and-change</strong> dimension: New Imperialism (post-1870) differed from earlier colonialism in scale, speed, and ideological justification, but shared the fundamental economic relationship of resource extraction from periphery to core. European-African commercial extraction dated to the 15th century; 19th-century colonialism accelerated and formalized it.</p><p>The concept of <strong>formal vs. informal empire</strong> is key: formal empire (direct political control, as in British India or French Algeria) and informal empire (economic dominance without annexation, as in British Latin America or semi-colonial China) both served metropolitan economic interests, just through different mechanisms.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>New Imperialism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Formal Empire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Informal Empire</span><span class='sg-kw'>Economic Causation</span><span class='sg-kw'>Technological Gap</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nationalist Rivalry</span></div>"}
],
7: [
  {"num":"Topic 7.1","title":"Shifting Power After 1900","body":"<p>During the early 20th century, a few major Western powers dominated the world through industrialization and overseas colonies. At the same time, traditional land empires were beginning to collapse. <strong>Three major empires fell:</strong> the Qing Dynasty (1911 revolution), the Russian Empire (1917 revolutions), and the Ottoman Empire (partitioned after WWI).</p><p><strong>New powers emerged:</strong> Japan, following the Meiji Restoration of 1868, underwent rapid modernization and became a major military and economic power, winning the Russo-Japanese War of 1905. The United States was also rising as a global player after acquiring the Philippines, Puerto Rico, and Guam in 1898.</p><p>Japan's growing power created tension with Western nations who refused to acknowledge Japan's equal status. At the 1919 Paris Peace Conference, Japan's proposal for a racial equality clause in the League of Nations was rejected, fueling resentment that fed interwar Japanese militarism. <strong>Okuma Shigenobu</strong> articulated this frustration in his 1921 essay, showing how Western racial discrimination paradoxically encouraged Japan to adopt the same 'civilizing mission' logic to justify its own imperialism in Asia.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Meiji Restoration</span><span class='sg-kw'>Qing Dynasty</span><span class='sg-kw'>Ottoman Decline</span><span class='sg-kw'>Racial Equality Proposal</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mexican Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Okuma Shigenobu</span><span class='sg-kw'>UNESCO 1949</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.2","title":"Causes of World War I (MAIN)","body":"<p>World War I erupted in July 1914 from a volatile mix of long-term tensions and a dramatic short-term trigger. The long-term causes are remembered as <strong>MAIN</strong>: Militarism, Alliances, Imperialism, and Nationalism.</p><ul><li><strong>Militarism</strong>: Arms race, especially between Britain and Germany in naval supremacy; military influence grew in Germany and Russia</li><li><strong>Alliances</strong>: Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) vs. Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy); any conflict could cascade</li><li><strong>Imperialism</strong>: Competition for colonies (Morocco Crises) strained relations and expanded the war's global scale</li><li><strong>Nationalism</strong>: Pan-Slavism in the Balkans clashed with Austria-Hungary's multi-ethnic empire; German nationalism demanded a larger role</li></ul><p>The short-term trigger: <strong>Archduke Franz Ferdinand</strong> killed June 28, 1914 by Gavrilo Princip in Sarajevo; Austria-Hungary's harsh ultimatum to Serbia pulled in Russia, then Germany, then France, then Britain.</p><p>The <strong>Treaty of Versailles</strong> (1919) ended WWI but planted seeds for WWII: Germany lost colonies, accepted sole War Guilt, paid massive reparations, and was severely demilitarized. <strong>Pyotr Durnovo</strong> predicted in 1914 that war would lead to social revolution in the losing country, a warning that proved remarkably accurate.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>MAIN</span><span class='sg-kw'>Triple Entente</span><span class='sg-kw'>Assassination of Franz Ferdinand</span><span class='sg-kw'>Treaty of Versailles</span><span class='sg-kw'>War Guilt Clause</span><span class='sg-kw'>Fourteen Points</span><span class='sg-kw'>Durnovo Memo</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.3","title":"Conducting WWI and Total War","body":"<p>World War I was characterized by <strong>total war</strong> strategies, nations mobilized all available resources and populations. This produced innovations that changed warfare permanently.</p><p><strong>Military technology:</strong> Machine guns led to trench warfare and massive casualties, the Battle of the Somme resulted in over a million losses. Poison gas (chlorine, mustard gas) was so devastating it was later banned by treaty. Tanks, airplanes, submarines (U-boats), and artillery were all used on massive industrial scales.</p><p><strong>Home front mobilization:</strong> Governments created production quotas, price controls, and ration systems. Women entered the industrial workforce in unprecedented numbers, in Britain alone, over 800,000 women entered industry.</p><p><strong>Propaganda:</strong> Germany's propaganda successfully blamed Russia for the war, mobilizing Russophobia (Watson, 2008). Britain used 'press lords' to suppress bad news: the sinking of HMS Audacious was never announced; civilians did not know the Battle of Jutland was happening (Goldfarb, 1978).</p><p>The war's immense strain on Russia led to the <strong>Russian Revolution</strong> of 1917, exactly as Durnovo had predicted.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Total War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Trench Warfare</span><span class='sg-kw'>Poison Gas</span><span class='sg-kw'>U-boats</span><span class='sg-kw'>Propaganda</span><span class='sg-kw'>Russian Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bolsheviks</span><span class='sg-kw'>Women in WWI</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.4","title":"The Economy in the Interwar Period","body":"<p>The 1920s saw rapid industrialization and consumer-driven growth, particularly in the United States. However, <strong>Germany faced hyperinflation in 1923</strong> due to war reparations from Versailles.</p><p>The boom ended with the <strong>Great Depression</strong>, triggered by the 1929 U.S. stock market crash. Between 1929-1933: U.S. GDP fell ~27%, unemployment reached 25%, international trade collapsed 50%, agricultural prices fell ~60%.</p><p><strong>Government responses varied:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>United States</strong>: FDR's New Deal used Keynesian government spending: Civilian Conservation Corps, Social Security, public works programs</li><li><strong>Soviet Union</strong>: Stalin's Five Year Plans centrally directed industrial modernization, causing immense human suffering</li><li><strong>Latin America</strong>: Import substitution and nationalization (Mexico's Pemex, 1938); Brazil's Vargas government enacted extensive worker protections</li><li><strong>Germany and Italy</strong>: Economic collapse fueled fascist movements</li></ul><p>The Brazilian government document under Vargas (c.1940) illustrates how even right-leaning governments adopted socialist-influenced worker protections, showing that socialist economic ideas spread across the political spectrum.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Great Depression</span><span class='sg-kw'>Keynesian Economics</span><span class='sg-kw'>Five Year Plans</span><span class='sg-kw'>New Deal</span><span class='sg-kw'>Import Substitution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Pemex</span><span class='sg-kw'>Vargas/Brazil</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.5","title":"Unresolved Tensions and Resistance After WWI","body":"<p>Between WWI and WWII, anti-imperial resistance intensified. The League of Nations mandate system transferred former German colonies to Western powers, despite promises of eventual self-governance, most mandates continued colonial exploitation.</p><p><strong>India:</strong> The Amritsar Massacre (1919): British troops killed hundreds of unarmed civilians, radicalized many Indians. Gandhi's Indian National Congress adopted <em>satyagraha</em> (nonviolent resistance). The Salt March (1930): 240 miles to make salt in defiance of colonial law, became an icon of civil disobedience.</p><p><strong>Vietnam:</strong> Ho Chi Minh petitioned Wilson for Vietnamese self-determination at the 1919 Paris Peace Conference. He was ignored. This experience radicalized him toward Marxism-Leninism as a framework for anti-colonial struggle.</p><p><strong>Turkey:</strong> Mustafa Kemal rejected the Allied partition of Anatolia, fought the Turkish War of Independence (1919-1923), and founded the secular Republic of Turkey, asserting that 'sovereignty belongs to the Turkish nation unconditionally.'</p><p><strong>Korea:</strong> Japan's Name Order of 1940 forced Koreans to adopt Japanese names, cultural imperialism designed to erase Korean identity and integrate Koreans into the Japanese Empire.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Indian National Congress</span><span class='sg-kw'>Satyagraha</span><span class='sg-kw'>Salt March</span><span class='sg-kw'>Ho Chi Minh</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mandate System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Amritsar Massacre</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mustafa Kemal</span><span class='sg-kw'>Name Order of 1940</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.6","title":"Causes and Conduct of World War II","body":"<p>WWII's causes are remembered with <strong>FIGURE</strong>: Fascist regimes, Imperialism, Global economic crisis, Unsustainable peace settlement (Versailles), Racism, Expansion of fascist regimes.</p><p>Japan invaded Manchuria (1931) and China (1937). Italy invaded Ethiopia (1935). Hitler annexed Austria (1938) and then the Sudetenland, which Britain and France yielded at the <strong>Munich Agreement</strong> (September 1938) in a policy of <strong>appeasement</strong>. Churchill called Munich 'a total and unmitigated defeat.' Hitler invaded the rest of Czechoslovakia six months later, then Poland on September 1, 1939, beginning WWII.</p><p>Hitler's 1937 speech to the Reichstag argued Germany needed colonies for 'foodstuffs and raw materials': showing imperialist aspirations drove Nazi policy. He explicitly connected this to Versailles, calling Germany's loss of colonies 'unreasonable.'</p><p><strong>Conduct:</strong> WWII was fought as a total war targeting civilians: the firebombing of Dresden and Tokyo, the Holocaust, and ultimately the <strong>atomic bombs</strong> on Hiroshima and Nagasaki (August 1945), killing approximately 200,000 people.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>FIGURE</span><span class='sg-kw'>Fascism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Appeasement</span><span class='sg-kw'>Munich Agreement</span><span class='sg-kw'>Blitzkrieg</span><span class='sg-kw'>Lebensraum</span><span class='sg-kw'>Atomic Bomb</span><span class='sg-kw'>Total War</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.7","title":"Mass Atrocities After 1900","body":"<p>The twentieth century saw mass atrocities on an unprecedented scale, consistently enabled by the same pattern: <strong>extremist ideology → dehumanizing propaganda → state-organized mass killing.</strong></p><p><strong>The Holocaust (1941-1945):</strong> Nazi Germany murdered approximately 6 million Jews and 6 million others using industrialized methods, gas chambers, crematoria, assembly-line processing in camps like Auschwitz. Enabled by the 'stab in the back' legend that falsely blamed Jews for WWI defeat, and by widespread local collaboration across occupied Europe.</p><p><strong>Armenian Genocide (1915-16):</strong> The Ottoman Empire killed 1-1.5 million Armenians during WWI, claiming Armenians were a fifth column supporting Russia. Considered one of the first modern genocides.</p><p><strong>Holodomor (1932-33):</strong> Soviet agricultural collectivization caused a famine killing millions of Ukrainians.</p><p><strong>Cambodian Genocide (1975-79):</strong> The Khmer Rouge killed approximately 1.5-2 million people: 25% of Cambodia's population.</p><p><strong>Rwandan Genocide (1994):</strong> Hutu extremists killed approximately 800,000 Tutsi in 100 days, enabled by decades of dehumanizing propaganda.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Holocaust</span><span class='sg-kw'>Auschwitz</span><span class='sg-kw'>Stab-in-the-Back Legend</span><span class='sg-kw'>Armenian Genocide</span><span class='sg-kw'>Holodomor</span><span class='sg-kw'>Khmer Rouge</span><span class='sg-kw'>Rwandan Genocide</span><span class='sg-kw'>David Olere</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.8","title":"Propaganda and Mobilization","body":"<p>Both World Wars required governments to mobilize entire civilian populations, not just armies. This made propaganda and media control essential tools of state power across the political spectrum.</p><p><strong>WWI:</strong> Germany manufactured stories of Russian atrocities to mobilize working-class anti-war sentiment into patriotic enlistment (Watson, 2008). Britain used 'press lords' to suppress bad news: maintaining the fiction that the war was going well (Goldfarb, 1978).</p><p><strong>WWII:</strong> The Soviet Union used posters linking Stalin to Lenin's legacy to build support for the Five Year Plans and 'Great Patriotic War.' Nazi Germany used Goebbels' propaganda ministry: film (Triumph of the Will), radio, and rallies. Western democracies used radio broadcasts (Churchill, FDR) and war bond campaigns.</p><p><strong>Key pattern:</strong> Democratic governments relied primarily on persuasion; totalitarian states used coercion: but both directed entire civilian societies toward the war effort through modern mass media technologies.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Watson/Goldfarb Sources</span><span class='sg-kw'>Soviet Posters</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nazi Propaganda</span><span class='sg-kw'>Goebbels</span><span class='sg-kw'>War Bond Campaigns</span><span class='sg-kw'>Total War Mobilization</span><span class='sg-kw'>UNESCO 1949</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 7.9","title":"Causation in Global Conflict","body":"<p>Topic 7.9 asks you to explain the <strong>causes and effects</strong> of the era's global conflicts. The First World War grew from <strong>nationalism</strong>, <strong>imperial rivalry</strong>, <strong>militarism</strong>, and an <strong>alliance system</strong> that turned the 1914 assassination in Sarajevo into a general war.</p><p>The peace settlement helped cause the next war. The <strong>Treaty of Versailles</strong> left Germany resentful, and the <strong>Great Depression</strong> helped bring fascist and militarist governments to power in Germany, Italy, and Japan. <strong>Appeasement</strong>, such as the 1938 Munich agreement, and the weak <strong>League of Nations</strong> failed to stop their expansion.</p><p>The wars had huge effects: <strong>total war</strong> mobilized whole societies, empires collapsed, mass atrocities such as the <strong>Holocaust</strong> took millions of lives, and the Second World War weakened European empires, setting the stage for <strong>decolonization</strong> and the <strong>Cold War</strong>.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Alliance System</span><span class='sg-kw'>Treaty of Versailles</span><span class='sg-kw'>Great Depression</span><span class='sg-kw'>Appeasement</span><span class='sg-kw'>League of Nations</span><span class='sg-kw'>Total War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Decolonization</span></div>"}
],
8: [
  {"num":"Topic 8.1","title":"Setting the Stage for the Cold War and Decolonization","body":"<p>World War II fundamentally restructured global power. Europe lay devastated; Britain and France emerged victorious but economically crippled. The United States and Soviet Union, the two powers least damaged relative to their size, emerged as <strong>superpowers</strong> with competing ideological visions for the postwar world.</p><p>The <strong>Yalta Conference (February 1945)</strong> planted the seeds of division: Roosevelt, Churchill, and Stalin agreed on Soviet dominance in Eastern Europe in exchange for Soviet entry into the Pacific war. As Soviet forces liberated Eastern Europe from Nazi occupation, they installed compliant communist governments. By 1946, Winston Churchill's <strong>'Iron Curtain' speech</strong> at Fulton, Missouri publicly named the East-West divide that had already formed.</p><p>The <strong>Truman Doctrine (1947)</strong> committed the United States to supporting 'free peoples resisting subjugation': operationalizing the containment strategy developed by diplomat George Kennan. The immediate trigger was communist insurgencies in Greece and Turkey; the implication was a global American commitment to resisting Soviet expansion wherever it appeared.</p><p>Simultaneously, European colonial empires weakened by WWII faced rising nationalist movements across Asia, Africa, and the Middle East, setting the stage for the decolonization wave that would reshape the globe over the following three decades.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Cold War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Yalta Conference</span><span class='sg-kw'>Iron Curtain</span><span class='sg-kw'>Truman Doctrine</span><span class='sg-kw'>Containment</span><span class='sg-kw'>George Kennan</span><span class='sg-kw'>Superpowers</span><span class='sg-kw'>Marshall Plan</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.2","title":"The Cold War","body":"<p>The Cold War was an ideological, political, and military competition between the <strong>United States (capitalism/democracy)</strong> and the <strong>Soviet Union (communism/authoritarianism)</strong> that dominated global politics from 1945 to 1991. Direct military conflict was avoided, both sides possessed nuclear weapons, but competition was intense across every other domain.</p><p>The <strong>nuclear arms race</strong> defined Cold War anxiety: the US used atomic bombs in 1945; the USSR tested its first nuclear weapon in 1949; both developed hydrogen bombs by 1953. By the 1960s, both possessed enough weapons to destroy civilization, the doctrine of <strong>Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD)</strong> meant any nuclear exchange would kill both sides.</p><p>The <strong>space race</strong> was both a military and propaganda competition. Sputnik (1957) proved Soviet missile technology could reach American cities; the Apollo moon landing (1969) asserted American technological superiority.</p><p>The <strong>Bandung Conference (1955)</strong> challenged Cold War bipolarity. Led by Sukarno (Indonesia), Nehru (India), Nkrumah (Ghana), and Nasser (Egypt), the 29 nations that gathered established the <strong>Non-Aligned Movement</strong>, asserting that newly independent nations need not choose between the two superpowers and that colonial solidarity transcended Cold War categories.</p><p><strong>NSC-68 (1950)</strong> framed the Cold War in existential terms: any communist gain anywhere was a loss for American security. This 'anywhere/everywhere' logic: later called the domino theory, justified massive military spending and intervention across the globe.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Nuclear Arms Race</span><span class='sg-kw'>MAD</span><span class='sg-kw'>Space Race</span><span class='sg-kw'>Sputnik</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bandung Conference</span><span class='sg-kw'>Non-Aligned Movement</span><span class='sg-kw'>NSC-68</span><span class='sg-kw'>Domino Theory</span><span class='sg-kw'>Berlin Wall</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.3","title":"Effects of the Cold War","body":"<p>The Cold War reshaped global military, political, and economic structures. Its most visible institutional effects were opposing military alliances: <strong>NATO (1949)</strong> bound Western democracies under American leadership; the <strong>Warsaw Pact (1955)</strong> formalized Soviet control of Eastern European satellite states. Both alliances maintained large standing armies and nuclear arsenals throughout the Cold War.</p><p>The <strong>Korean War (1950-53)</strong> was the Cold War's first major armed conflict. North Korea's Soviet-backed invasion of South Korea prompted US-led UN intervention. The war ended in armistice with Korea still divided at the 38th parallel, an outcome that established the precedent of 'limited war' in the nuclear age: the US would not escalate to nuclear weapons or attack China directly.</p><p>The <strong>Cuban Missile Crisis (October 1962)</strong> brought the world closest to nuclear war. The discovery of Soviet missiles in Cuba triggered a 13-day confrontation resolved only through direct Kennedy-Khrushchev negotiation. The crisis produced the Moscow-Washington <strong>hotline (1963)</strong> and a partial nuclear test ban treaty, demonstrating that both sides feared escalation.</p><p>The <strong>Vietnam War</strong> became the defining Cold War proxy conflict of the 1960s-70s. American forces withdrew in 1973 after massive casualties on all sides; North Vietnam unified the country under communism in 1975, proving that military intervention could not guarantee political outcomes.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>NATO</span><span class='sg-kw'>Warsaw Pact</span><span class='sg-kw'>Korean War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cuban Missile Crisis</span><span class='sg-kw'>Hotline</span><span class='sg-kw'>Vietnam War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Proxy War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Limited War</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.4","title":"Spread of Communism After 1900","body":"<p>Communism spread beyond the Soviet Union through a combination of revolutionary movements, Soviet support, and anti-colonial nationalism that found in Marxism-Leninism a framework for opposing Western imperialism.</p><p>The <strong>Chinese Communist Revolution (1949)</strong> was the most consequential. Mao Zedong's forces defeated US-backed Nationalists after a long civil war, establishing the <strong>People's Republic of China</strong>. Mao proclaimed the Chinese people had 'stood up': ending the Century of Humiliation. The <strong>Great Leap Forward (1958-62)</strong>, a forced collectivization campaign, caused a famine killing 15-55 million people. The <strong>Cultural Revolution (1966-76)</strong> purged perceived enemies of the revolution.</p><p><strong>Ho Chi Minh</strong> led Vietnamese independence from France. His 1945 Declaration of Independence quoted Jefferson while condemning French colonialism, strategically appealing to American anti-colonial ideals. After defeating France at <strong>Dien Bien Phu (1954)</strong>, Vietnam was temporarily divided. Ho's North Vietnam eventually reunified the country after defeating American intervention in 1975.</p><p>Communist and socialist movements gained ground across Latin America and Africa. Cuba's <strong>Fidel Castro</strong> (1959 revolution) aligned with the USSR, triggering the Bay of Pigs and Cuban Missile Crisis. Land reform movements in Ethiopia, Vietnam, India's Kerala state, and elsewhere reflected communist and socialist influence on postcolonial governance.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Mao Zedong</span><span class='sg-kw'>PRC</span><span class='sg-kw'>Great Leap Forward</span><span class='sg-kw'>Cultural Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Ho Chi Minh</span><span class='sg-kw'>Dien Bien Phu</span><span class='sg-kw'>Viet Minh</span><span class='sg-kw'>Castro</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.5","title":"Decolonization After 1900","body":"<p>Between 1945 and 1975, over 50 new nations emerged as European colonial empires dissolved. WWII accelerated decolonization by exhausting European powers, shattering the myth of European invincibility (Japan conquered most of Southeast Asia in weeks), and raising nationalist expectations through wartime rhetoric about freedom and self-determination.</p><p><strong>India (1947)</strong> was the most consequential early independence. Gandhi's Congress Party had built a mass nationalist movement through <em>satyagraha</em> (nonviolent resistance). Independence came simultaneously with the violent <strong>Partition</strong> into Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan, displacing 10-20 million people and killing up to 2 million in communal violence.</p><p><strong>Ghana (1957)</strong> was the first sub-Saharan African nation to achieve independence. Kwame Nkrumah's success through political organizing, not armed struggle: inspired the wave of African decolonization. The <strong>'Year of Africa' (1960)</strong> saw 17 nations gain independence in a single year.</p><p>Not all decolonization was peaceful. The <strong>Algerian War of Independence (1954-62)</strong> killed over 1 million Algerians as France tried to retain its settler colony. Angola, Kenya (Mau Mau uprising), and Mozambique also saw armed independence struggles against Portuguese and British colonial rule.</p><p>Decolonization paths varied: negotiated independence (India, Ghana), armed struggle (Algeria, Angola), and UN trust territory transitions. In all cases, WWII's weakening of European powers and the Cold War superpowers' (at least rhetorical) support for self-determination accelerated the process.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Partition of India</span><span class='sg-kw'>Kwame Nkrumah</span><span class='sg-kw'>Ghana</span><span class='sg-kw'>Year of Africa</span><span class='sg-kw'>Algerian Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mau Mau</span><span class='sg-kw'>Negotiated Independence</span><span class='sg-kw'>Armed Struggle</span><span class='sg-kw'>Satyagraha</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.6","title":"Newly Independent States","body":"<p>Independence brought the enormous challenge of building functioning states. Newly independent nations inherited colonial structures designed to serve imperial interests, not their own populations.</p><p><strong>Inherited problems:</strong> Colonial borders (drawn at the Berlin Conference 1884-85) divided ethnic groups or united hostile peoples, creating internal conflicts. Economies were structured around raw material export. Educational systems had deliberately limited African and Asian access to higher education. Infrastructure connected interior resources to coastal ports, not population centers to each other.</p><p>Several newly independent states faced immediate <strong>partition crises</strong>: India-Pakistan (1947), Israel-Palestine (1948), Korea (1945), and Vietnam (1954): each creating refugee crises, border wars, and ongoing conflicts that defined postcolonial politics.</p><p>Leaders like India's <strong>Jawaharlal Nehru</strong> and Egypt's <strong>Gamal Abdel Nasser</strong> pursued state-directed economic development: five-year plans, nationalization of key industries (Suez Canal 1956), and import substitution. These strategies reflected both socialist influence and the practical reality that private investment was insufficient for rapid development.</p><p><strong>Post-colonial migration</strong> maintained cultural and economic ties between former colonies and metropoles: South Asians to Britain, Algerians and West Africans to France, Filipinos to the United States. Former colonizers became multicultural societies shaped by their colonial histories.</p><p><strong>Neocolonialism</strong>: economic dependency on former colonial powers, limited genuine sovereignty for many new states, even after formal independence.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Nehru</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nasser</span><span class='sg-kw'>Neocolonialism</span><span class='sg-kw'>Berlin Conference Borders</span><span class='sg-kw'>Partition</span><span class='sg-kw'>State-Directed Development</span><span class='sg-kw'>Post-Colonial Migration</span><span class='sg-kw'>Five-Year Plans</span><span class='sg-kw'>Suez Crisis</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.7","title":"Global Resistance to Established Power Structures After 1900","body":"<p>The postwar period saw a global wave of resistance movements challenging racial hierarchy, colonial rule, and political oppression, often drawing on shared language of universal human rights and dignity.</p><p>The <strong>anti-apartheid movement</strong> in South Africa challenged the apartheid system (1948-1994) that legally enforced racial segregation. The <strong>African National Congress (ANC)</strong>, led by <strong>Nelson Mandela</strong>, used nonviolent resistance until the <strong>Sharpeville Massacre (1960)</strong>: when police killed 69 peaceful protesters, prompted the ANC to adopt limited armed tactics. Mandela was imprisoned for 27 years. International sanctions and internal resistance eventually forced negotiations, producing democratic elections in 1994.</p><p>The American <strong>civil rights movement</strong> challenged legal racial segregation in the United States. <strong>Martin Luther King Jr.</strong>'s strategy of nonviolent direct action, bus boycotts, sit-ins, marches, drew explicitly on Gandhi's methods. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) and 'I Have a Dream' speech articulated universal human dignity as the movement's moral foundation. The Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965) dismantled legal segregation.</p><p>Gandhi directly inspired both King and Mandela, creating a visible <strong>transnational tradition of nonviolent resistance</strong>. However, other movements (FLN in Algeria, ANC after Sharpeville, Mau Mau in Kenya) concluded that nonviolence alone was insufficient against brutal colonial suppression.</p><p><strong>Anti-nuclear movements</strong> (Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament in Britain, American anti-war protests during Vietnam) also challenged established power structures through mass civil action.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Apartheid</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nelson Mandela</span><span class='sg-kw'>ANC</span><span class='sg-kw'>Sharpeville Massacre</span><span class='sg-kw'>Martin Luther King Jr.</span><span class='sg-kw'>Civil Rights Act 1964</span><span class='sg-kw'>Nonviolent Resistance</span><span class='sg-kw'>Gandhi's Global Influence</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.8","title":"End of the Cold War","body":"<p>The Cold War ended not with a bang but with the internal collapse of the Soviet system. By the 1980s, the USSR faced compounding crises: economic stagnation, the drain of the <strong>Soviet-Afghan War (1979-89)</strong>: its 'Vietnam': Chernobyl (1986), and the visible gap between Soviet austerity and Western consumer prosperity visible through radio and television.</p><p>Mikhail <strong>Gorbachev</strong> (General Secretary from 1985) launched two transformative reforms. <strong>Glasnost</strong> (openness) allowed public criticism of Soviet history and institutions. <strong>Perestroika</strong> (restructuring) introduced limited market mechanisms into the planned economy. Gorbachev intended these to save the Soviet system; instead, openness revealed its failures and released pent-up nationalist and democratic demands across Soviet republics.</p><p>Critically, Gorbachev repudiated the <strong>Brezhnev Doctrine</strong> (1968): the principle that the USSR would use military force to preserve communist governments in Eastern Europe. Without Soviet tanks as backstop, communist governments across Eastern Europe collapsed within months:</p><ul><li><strong>Poland</strong>: Solidarity movement won free elections (August 1989)</li><li><strong>East Germany</strong>: <strong>Berlin Wall fell November 9, 1989</strong>, as border guards stood aside</li><li><strong>Czechoslovakia</strong>: Velvet Revolution (November 1989)</li><li><strong>Romania</strong>: Ceausescu executed (December 1989)</li></ul><p><strong>German reunification</strong> followed in October 1990. The Soviet Union itself dissolved December 25, 1991, when Gorbachev resigned and 15 republics became independent nations. The Cold War was over.</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Gorbachev</span><span class='sg-kw'>Glasnost</span><span class='sg-kw'>Perestroika</span><span class='sg-kw'>Afghan War</span><span class='sg-kw'>Brezhnev Doctrine</span><span class='sg-kw'>Berlin Wall Falls</span><span class='sg-kw'>Solidarity</span><span class='sg-kw'>Velvet Revolution</span><span class='sg-kw'>Soviet Dissolution</span></div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 8.9","title":"Causation in the Age of the Cold War and Decolonization","body":"<p>Topic 8.9 asks you to connect the <strong>causes and effects</strong> of the Cold War and decolonization. The <strong>Second World War</strong> left the United States and the Soviet Union as rival superpowers and exhausted Europe's colonial empires, while nationalist movements in Asia and Africa grew stronger.</p><p>The two processes shaped each other. Fear of <strong>nuclear war</strong> (mutually assured destruction) kept the superpowers from fighting directly, so they competed through <strong>proxy wars</strong> in Korea, Vietnam, and Angola. Many newly independent states tried to avoid the rivalry through the <strong>Non-Aligned Movement</strong> launched at <strong>Bandung (1955)</strong>, while others accepted aid from one side.</p><p>The Cold War ended when <strong>economic stagnation</strong>, the costly <strong>Soviet-Afghan War</strong>, and Gorbachev's <strong>glasnost and perestroika</strong> led to the fall of the Berlin Wall (1989) and the collapse of the Soviet Union (1991).</p><div class='sg-kws'><span class='sg-kw'>Superpowers</span><span class='sg-kw'>Proxy Wars</span><span class='sg-kw'>Mutually Assured Destruction</span><span class='sg-kw'>Non-Aligned Movement</span><span class='sg-kw'>Bandung Conference</span><span class='sg-kw'>Glasnost</span><span class='sg-kw'>Perestroika</span></div>"}
],
9: [
  {"num":"Overview","title":"1. What Is Globalization?","body":"<p>Globalization refers to the increasing <strong>interconnectedness of the world's economies, cultures, politics, and populations</strong> through the exchange of goods, services, ideas, and people. It accelerated dramatically after World War II and especially after 1990 with the internet, the collapse of the Soviet Union, and the expansion of free trade.</p>\n<p>Globalization is not new: earlier waves occurred during the Columbian Exchange, the era of industrialization, and the Age of Imperialism. But the post-WWII era is distinguished by its <strong>speed, scale, and depth</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Economic globalization:</strong> global trade, MNCs, financial markets</li>\n<li><strong>Cultural globalization:</strong> spread of ideas, media, and practices</li>\n<li><strong>Political globalization:</strong> international institutions, NGOs, global governance</li>\n<li><strong>Technological globalization:</strong> internet, telecommunications, transportation</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">interconnectedness</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">post-WWII</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">free trade</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">MNC</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Economy","title":"2. Economic Globalization &amp; Inequality","body":"<p>The <strong>WTO (1995)</strong> replaced the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT), creating enforceable global trade rules. Regional agreements like <strong>NAFTA (1994)</strong> further reduced barriers.</p>\n<p>Multinational corporations (MNCs) became dominant economic actors. <strong>Nike</strong> designs shoes in Oregon, manufactures in Vietnam, and sells globally. This \"global value chain\" creates jobs in developing countries but often at low wages and in poor conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>China's rise</strong> is the defining economic story of globalization: 800 million people lifted out of poverty in 40 years through export-led growth. But this also hollowed out manufacturing in the U.S., Europe, and other developed economies, the <strong>deindustrialization</strong> of the Rust Belt.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free trade → lower prices for consumers, but job losses in developed nations</li>\n<li>MNCs → development AND exploitation; \"race to the bottom\" on labor standards</li>\n<li>Microfinance (Grameen Bank) → market-based poverty reduction</li>\n<li>Inequality: within-country gaps widened even as between-country gaps narrowed</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">WTO</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">NAFTA</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">deindustrialization</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">outsourcing</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Grameen Bank</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Gini coefficient</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Technology","title":"3. Technology &amp; Communication","body":"<p>The <strong>internet (World Wide Web, 1991)</strong> transformed how information spreads, how commerce operates, and how people organize politically. Combined with smartphones, it made global real-time communication available to billions.</p>\n<p><strong>Container shipping</strong> (1950s–60s) was equally transformative: reducing the cost of moving goods by ~90% and enabling the global supply chains that made modern manufacturing possible.</p>\n<p>However, technology's benefits are <strong>unevenly distributed</strong>. The \"digital divide\" separates those with internet access from those without, often mapping onto existing inequalities of wealth, gender, and geography.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Social media enabled the Arab Spring (2010–11) but also disinformation</li>\n<li>mRNA vaccines demonstrated rapid-response biotech capability (COVID-19)</li>\n<li>Automation threatens jobs: even in developing nations previously reliant on cheap labor</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">WWW</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">digital divide</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">container shipping</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">mRNA</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">automation</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Institutions","title":"4. Multinational Institutions &amp; Global Governance","body":"<p>Post-WWII, world leaders created a web of institutions to manage globalization and prevent future catastrophic war. The <strong>United Nations (1945)</strong> provided a forum for diplomacy; the <strong>IMF and World Bank</strong> stabilized economies; the <strong>WTO</strong> governed trade.</p>\n<p>The <strong>European Union</strong> represents the deepest regional integration, a common currency, free movement, and shared laws. <strong>Brexit (2016)</strong> showed that integration can reverse when populations feel it benefits elites more than ordinary workers.</p>\n<p>Critics of institutions like the IMF argue that <strong>\"structural adjustment\" programs</strong>: which require austerity and privatization in exchange for loans, perpetuate dependency rather than promoting genuine development.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>UN peacekeeping missions stabilized some post-conflict zones but failed in others (Rwanda 1994)</li>\n<li>ICC (2002) marks the first permanent international criminal tribunal</li>\n<li>NATO expanded post-Cold War; Russia-Ukraine war (2022) reinvigorated its purpose</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">UN</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">IMF</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">EU</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">NATO</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">ICC</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">structural adjustment</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Brexit</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Culture","title":"5. Cultural Globalization &amp; Resistance","body":"<p>Culture spreads alongside goods and information. <strong>Americanization</strong>: the global spread of U.S. culture (fast food, music, films, fashion), is the most visible form of cultural globalization, but it coexists with cultural hybridity and local resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>Glocalization</strong> describes how global products adapt to local cultures (McDonald's in India serving the McAloo Tikki; K-pop blending Korean and Western pop). This shows globalization is not simply one-directional Westernization.</p>\n<p>Some communities actively resist cultural globalization. France mandates French on radio. Iran restricts Western media. Indigenous peoples worldwide fight for language and cultural preservation, supported by the <strong>UN Declaration on Rights of Indigenous Peoples (2007)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">Americanization</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">glocalization</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">hybridity</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">K-pop</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">UNESCO</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">cultural imperialism</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Disease","title":"6. Disease &amp; Global Health","body":"<p>Globalization accelerates disease spread by increasing the speed and volume of human movement. <strong>HIV/AIDS</strong> (identified 1981) spread via global travel networks, eventually infecting ~38 million people worldwide. Sub-Saharan Africa bore the greatest burden.</p>\n<p><strong>SARS (2003)</strong> was the first disease to spread globally via air travel, 29 countries in weeks. It served as a warning. <strong>COVID-19</strong> showed what an uncontained pandemic looks like in a hyper-connected world: ~7 million official deaths, global supply chain collapse, and a race for vaccines that exposed stark inequalities in global health access.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WHO: coordinates global health responses but lacks enforcement power</li>\n<li>Antiretroviral drugs transformed HIV from fatal to manageable, but only for those with access</li>\n<li>\"Diseases of globalization\": obesity, diabetes spreading with Westernized diets</li>\n<li>Vaccine nationalism: wealthy nations secured COVID vaccines first, leaving poor nations behind</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">HIV/AIDS</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">COVID-19</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">SARS</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">WHO</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">antiretroviral</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">vaccine nationalism</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Reform","title":"7. Reform, Resistance &amp; Backlash","body":"<p>Globalization produces both reform movements (calling for more just and sustainable globalization) and backlash movements (demanding less globalization). The <strong>anti-globalization movement</strong> gained visibility at the 1999 WTO Seattle protests, arguing that global trade rules prioritized corporate profits over workers and the environment.</p>\n<p>The <strong>environmental movement</strong> achieved global governance milestones: Kyoto Protocol (1997) and the Paris Agreement (2015). Climate activism became a truly global youth movement with figures like <strong>Greta Thunberg</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nationalist and populist backlash in the 2010s, <strong>Brexit, Trump, European far-right parties</strong>, reflects the \"losers\" of globalization: workers in deindustrialized regions, communities threatened by immigration, and citizens who feel sovereignty has been ceded to faceless international bodies.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">WTO Seattle</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Paris Agreement</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Brexit</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">NGO</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Arab Spring</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">populism</span>\n</div>"},
  {"num":"Topic 9.9","title":"8. Continuity &amp; Change in a Globalized World","body":"<p>Topic 9.9 asks what changed and what stayed the same after 1900. <strong>Changes:</strong> life expectancy more than doubled thanks to vaccines, antibiotics, and the Green Revolution; communication and transportation technology connected the world almost instantly; women and minority groups won new rights; and economic power shifted toward Asia as <strong>China</strong>, India, and the Asian Tigers grew.</p>\n<p><strong>Continuities:</strong> large gaps in wealth between the Global North and South persisted; many African and Latin American economies still depend on exporting raw materials, as in the age of imperialism; <strong>nationalism</strong> remained powerful, resurging in the 2010s; and states continued to compete for resources and power even as international institutions grew.</p>\n<div class=\"sg-kws\">\n<span class=\"sg-kw\">Life expectancy</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Global inequality</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Rise of China</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Export economies</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Nationalism</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Continuity</span><span class=\"sg-kw\">Change</span>\n</div>"}
]
};
